           --------------------Here is the program that reads the C# code, identifies all comments that start with "@@@", and outputs `Debug.Assert` statements for them.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;
using System.Linq;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        string input = @"
// Some regular comment
public static int Sum(int x, int y)
{
    sum = x + y;
    // @@@ sum is greater than or equal to x and y 
    return sum;
}

public static string SomeMethod(string s, int y)
{
    // regular comment
    string str = s + y.ToString();
    // @@@ length of y should be smaller than x
    return str;
}
";

        Console.WriteLine(input);

        const string pattern = @"(\/\/ @@@(?<AssertMessage>[\s\S]*)\n)";
        var matches = Regex.Matches(input, pattern);

        foreach (Match match in matches)
        {
            Console.WriteLine(match.ToString().TrimStart('/'));
            string assertMessage = match.Groups["AssertMessage"].Value.Trim();
            Console.WriteLine("    Debug.Assert({0});", assertMessage);
        }
    }
}
```

You can replace the `input` constant with any C# code you want to read, then run it. The Output will be `Debug.Assert(...)` lines for all comments starting with `@@@`. Note that you have to include the using System.Text.RegularExpressions; directive for the program to work.--------------------